{"body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states, \u201cThe object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction,\u201d is required to return {\"a\":1} for reply one and {\"b\":2} for reply two. Reply one is:\n```python\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n{\"b\":2}\nReply two is:\n{\"b\":2}\nThe rule requires {\"a\":1} for reply one because the object {\"a\":1} is inside a code fence (a ```python fence) and is the last fenced object; it requires {\"b\":2} for reply two because there is no fenced object and the last top-level object is {\"b\":2}. The check, however, gives the same answer for both: its fence regex is '```(?:json|JSON)?\\s*(.*?)```', so the ```python fence is not recognized, the fenced pool is empty, and the last top-level object in reply one is {\"b\":2}, matching reply two. This conjecture would be false if running recover_json_object on reply one returned {\"a\":1} (or any answer different from {\"b\":2}), or if the documented rule were not the one quoted."}